{"subjectId":"javascript","id":"classes","title":"Classes","group":"Prototypes & Classes","order":32,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>class</code> is clean syntax over prototypes — with real extras: public and <code>#private</code> fields, static members, static blocks, accessors, and strict mode by default.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-code\"></i>Anatomy of a class</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>class BankAccount {\n  static bankName = \"JS Bank\";        // static field (on the class)\n  static #count = 0;                  // private static field\n  owner;                              // public instance field\n  #balance = 0;                       // private instance field\n\n  constructor(owner, deposit = 0) {\n    this.owner = owner;\n    this.#balance = deposit;\n    BankAccount.#count++;\n  }\n  deposit(amount) {                   // method on BankAccount.prototype\n    if (amount &lt;= 0) throw new RangeError(\"amount must be positive\");\n    this.#balance += amount;\n    return this;                      // enables chaining\n  }\n  get balance() { return this.#balance; }      // accessor\n  #audit() { return `audited ${this.owner}`; } // private method\n  report() { return this.#audit(); }\n  static count() { return BankAccount.#count; }\n}\n\nconst acc = new BankAccount(\"Asha\", 100);\nacc.deposit(50).deposit(25);\nconsole.log(acc.balance, acc.report(), acc);\nconsole.log(BankAccount.bankName, BankAccount.count(), typeof BankAccount);</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>175 audited Asha BankAccount { owner: 'Asha' }\nJS Bank 1 function</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-lock\"></i>Private really means private</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>class Secret {\n  #code = 1234;\n  static isSecret(obj) { return #code in obj; }   // brand check (ES2022)\n}\nconst s = new Secret();\nconsole.log(s.code, Object.keys(s), JSON.stringify(s));\nconsole.log(Secret.isSecret(s), Secret.isSecret({ code: 1 }));</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>undefined [] {}\ntrue false</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Accessing <code>s.#code</code> outside the class body is a <strong>SyntaxError</strong> — the program does not even parse. Before <code>#</code>, the convention was a leading underscore (<code>this._code</code>), which is only a hint.</p>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrow-right\"></i>Class fields with arrow functions</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>class Button {\n  label = \"Save\";\n  handleArrow = () =&gt; `clicked ${this.label}`;   // per-instance, this is bound\n  handleMethod() { return `clicked ${this?.label}`; }\n}\nconst b = new Button();\nconst { handleArrow, handleMethod } = b;          // detach both\nconsole.log(handleArrow(), \"|\", handleMethod.call(undefined));\nconsole.log(Object.hasOwn(b, \"handleArrow\"), Object.hasOwn(b, \"handleMethod\"));</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>clicked Save | clicked undefined\ntrue false</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Arrow-function fields fix lost-<code>this</code> in callbacks, but each instance gets its own copy (more memory) and they cannot be overridden via <code>super</code> — trade-offs worth mentioning.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-bolt\"></i>Static blocks and getters</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>class Config {\n  static defaults;\n  static {\n    // runs once when the class is defined\n    Config.defaults = Object.freeze({ retries: 3, timeout: 1000 });\n  }\n  static get retries() { return Config.defaults.retries; }\n}\nconsole.log(Config.retries, Config.defaults);</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>3 { retries: 3, timeout: 1000 }</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-info-circle\"></i>Classes are special functions</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Behaviour</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Must be called with <code>new</code></td><td><code>BankAccount()</code> → <code>TypeError: Class constructor … cannot be invoked without 'new'</code></td></tr><tr><td>Hoisted into the TDZ</td><td>Using a class before its declaration throws <code>ReferenceError</code></td></tr><tr><td>Strict mode</td><td>The whole class body is strict</td></tr><tr><td>Methods are non-enumerable</td><td>They do not show up in <code>for…in</code> over instances</td></tr><tr><td>Fields run per instance</td><td>Initialised before the constructor body (after <code>super()</code> in subclasses)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>class Foo {}\ntry {\n  Foo();\n} catch (e) {\n  console.log(e.message);\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>Class constructor Foo cannot be invoked without 'new'</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Are JavaScript classes real classes?</summary><div><p>They are syntax over prototypal inheritance: a class is a constructor function, methods go on <code>Class.prototype</code>, and <code>extends</code> links prototype chains. But they add real semantics — mandatory <code>new</code>, strict mode, <code>#private</code> fields, <code>super</code>, and static blocks.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you make a property private?</summary><div><p>Use a <code>#field</code> (hard privacy, enforced by the language, ES2022). Older options: closures (factory functions), <code>WeakMap</code> keyed by the instance, or the <code>_underscore</code> convention (not enforced).</p></div></details></section>"}